    "description": "Recreational running in Iran includes informal training networks and organized road events. Research and event reporting reveal both opportunities for participation and constraints involving injury, event access, and women’s public presence.",
    "tags": ["Running", "Community sport", "Recreational exercise", "Road races", "Women in sport"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:31:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:31:17Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Running in Iran: Recreational Communities and Road Events",
      "description": "Explore recreational running in Iran through research in Tehran, Mashhad and Shiraz, and documented road events in Tehran and Kish."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "recreational-networks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Running beyond elite competition",
        "content": "Iran’s running communities extend beyond national-team athletes. A research paper published in 2021 recruited recreational runners in Tehran, Mashhad, and Shiraz through social media, clubs, gyms, university departments, and sports shops. These recruitment channels offer a concrete glimpse of the networks surrounding ordinary training. They show several ways runners could be reached, rather than providing a census of clubs or participants. [s1]\n\nThe researchers distinguished recreational runners from athletes classified as elite by the athletics federation. Their findings therefore concern people with an established running routine, not everyone who occasionally jogs. This distinction is important when interpreting research: a sample of committed participants cannot establish how common running is across Iran or how easily a newcomer can join a local group. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-and-wellbeing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What injury research can explain",
        "content": "The same study investigated self-reported lower-limb injuries alongside training characteristics, sleep, perceived health, and psychological factors. Its findings frame running injuries as a multifactorial subject rather than simply a consequence of distance. However, the cross-sectional design identifies associations; it cannot establish whether a particular factor caused an injury. Recruitment by convenience also limits generalization. These results are useful context for understanding recreational sport, rather than individualized medical guidance. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tehran-event",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Public events and unequal access",
        "content": "Organized races make running visible, but event conditions shape who can participate and where. Reporting on Tehran’s international race on 7 April 2017 described men running through western Tehran while women ran separately inside the Azadi sports complex. The event therefore offered different experiences of public space to male and female participants. [s2]\n\nInternational access was another issue. The federation’s head said there was no nationality ban, while American applicants reported being unable to obtain visas. Preserving both accounts matters: an organizer’s statement about eligibility and an entrant’s practical ability to arrive are different measures of openness. This historical example should not be treated as a statement of current visa policy. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "kish-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation and public expression",
        "content": "A later event on Kish, an island in the Persian Gulf, again illustrated the relationship between running and regulation. RFE/RL reported that women and men participated in separate races on 5 December 2025. The following day, the judiciary announced the arrest of two organizers after some women ran without the mandatory head covering. The report attributed the authorities’ justification to judicial statements and acknowledged uncertainty about the organizers’ custodial situation. [s3]\n\nThese episodes show why a race’s existence does not, by itself, demonstrate equal access or settled rules. Community running is best understood through both ordinary training networks and the conditions surrounding public events. Neither prominent races nor research samples establish a continuous national participation trend. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
